From 5b00b364c92dbd32c30303591546519d6e5b4374 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 19 Jul 2026 16:13:52 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 229/273] drm/sun4i: Add hardware RCQ submission for DE33

DE33 uses a register command queue to latch display state
atomically. Extend the register-shadow helper to build an RCQ head list,
copy dirty regions into 32-byte-aligned coherent staging blocks and
trigger the queue at atomic commit. Register offsets are relative to
the DE bus window.

The queue follows the vendor update protocol: wait until the previous
fetch was consumed before touching the head list, gate fetches while
the staging memory is inconsistent, and retire consumed dirty flags so
a spurious refetch applies nothing. If the hardware never consumed the
previous update, fall back to writing the registers with the CPU. An
engine sync hook exposes the same wait to other update sources sharing
the fetch engine, like the writeback queue.

Convert mixer-global and blender state to the same shadow interface. Older
engines retain the software fallback, which writes dirty shadow regions
to MMIO at the end of the commit. No display-engine state is read back
or updated with read-modify-write cycles.

Blender registers are now addressed relative to their shadow region, so
drop the base argument from the blend macros. The few registers outside
shadow regions use writel() directly.

Signed-off-by: Jernej Skrabec <jernej.skrabec@gmail.com>
